---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/klaviyo-vs-postscript';
const title = 'Klaviyo vs Postscript: 2026 Shopify Comparison';
const desc = "Compare Shopify event and profile workflows with Postscript capture, replies and channel ownership; plan a measured SMS evaluation.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Which should own SMS?",
    "answer": "Choose from demonstrated capture, events, replies and operating responsibility in the eligible account. Klaviyo can suit shared email/SMS journey work; Postscript can suit a dedicated text program. There is no universal winner."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I use both?",
    "answer": "Yes, evaluate the documented integration prerequisites. Click and popup property sync do not by themselves prove permission or purchase suppression across waiting queues."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should I compare pricing?",
    "answer": "Quote the same profiles, sends, countries, segments, MMS and peak workload. Include modules, carrier fees, integration work and reply staffing rather than relying on a stale starting price."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does the proposed test prove compliance or lift?",
    "answer": "No. Validate permission and destination requirements with the responsible reviewer. Seed tests check operations; an eligible holdout and uncertainty analysis are needed for a causal lift estimate."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a></nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl"><p class="badge">Head-to-head</p><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Klaviyo vs Postscript for Shopify</h1><p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">Choose around the work your team will own: customer events and email/SMS journeys in Klaviyo, or a dedicated Postscript SMS program with capture and reply operations. A split setup can be useful when its data contract is explicit. Neither architecture establishes better conversion or compliance. The streetwear, beauty and supplement examples below are proposed evaluations, not observed merchant results.</p></header>
    <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl"><TldrBox picks={[{"label": "Shared customer journeys", "tool": "Klaviyo", "reason": "Evaluate Shopify events and profiles alongside email and SMS requirements."}, {"label": "Dedicated text acquisition", "tool": "Postscript", "reason": "Evaluate keywords, popups and an owned reply queue."}, {"label": "Split channels", "tool": "Either architecture", "reason": "Choose from demonstrated coverage and maintenance cost."}]} /></div>
    <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Klaviyo</th><th>Postscript</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Event model</td><td>Shopify integration plus configured onsite tracking</td><td>Validate the Shopify triggers needed by your SMS journeys</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Capture</td><td>Evaluate existing forms and channel permissions</td><td>Keywords and popups can feed a configured Klaviyo integration</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Shared data</td><td>Inspect the actual matched profile and event</td><td>Click, coupon and popup property sync have distinct prerequisites</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Replies</td><td>Confirm the intended sender and account routing</td><td>Dedicated toll-free number supports subscriber responses</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cost</td><td>Quote eligible profiles, email and SMS requirements</td><td>Quote plan, message usage and operator work</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Start with events, not a platform rank</h2>
      <p>Klaviyo’s <a class="link" href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115005080407">Shopify integration guide</a> describes subscriber syncing and onsite setup. Its <a class="link" href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115005080447">data reference</a> distinguishes Shopify events from app-embed tracking. This is a positive fit for a beauty store that wants purchase, browse and signup information available to customer journeys. Enable and inspect the required tracking; an anonymous visit is not automatically a usable profile event.</p><p>Postscript is a positive fit for a streetwear team building text acquisition around drop keywords and popups. Its <a class="link" href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13564129-create-a-keyword">keyword guide</a> documents configurable replies and actions, with keyword allowances varying by plan. Evaluate capture source, disclosure version and permission separately from the keyword’s attributed sales. A keyword count is not an incrementality result.</p>
      <h2>Proposed capture-to-profile fixture</h2>
      <p>Use separate seed identities for a popup collecting email and phone, a phone-only keyword subscriber, an existing Klaviyo customer with a new phone number, and a withdrawn subscriber. Keep email and SMS permissions separate. Preserve the source, timestamp, exact disclosure and intended purpose from checkout, popup or keyword capture; importing a list does not create new permission.</p><p>Postscript documents <a class="link" href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13564183-send-custom-properties-from-postscript-s-popups-to-subscriber-profiles-in-klaviyo">popup property forwarding</a> when email collection, integration and destination list are configured. Its <a class="link" href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13564206-send-postscript-click-activity-to-klaviyo">click activity sync</a> needs an email address to match a Klaviyo profile. Inspect the matched profile, source property and event after each seed action. Phone-only activity must not be treated as proof that an email profile was updated. Test repeated submissions and changed addresses for duplicates before using the properties in live branches.</p>
      <h2>Proposed cart and reply fixture</h2>
      <p>For the streetwear cart, assign one owner to SMS recovery and one to email education. Use configurable waits based on the store’s promise and customer context, rather than a universal twenty-minute or twenty-four-hour rule. Place a seed purchase while recovery is waiting, withdraw permission in another seed, replay the checkout event and delay its purchase event. Inspect both tools’ waiting queues, cancellation and event timestamps. These are acceptance checks; cross-tool purchase suppression is not established by click syncing.</p><p>Postscript’s <a class="link" href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13564366-what-is-my-phone-number-subscribers-can-text-to-opt-into-my-sms">number guide</a> describes a brand-specific toll-free number and subscriber responses. Assign a person to ordinary questions, STOP/HELP handling and escalation before advertising conversational recovery. In both candidate accounts, demonstrate the intended sender, supported destination, quiet hours/local time, withdrawal and support handoff using eligible seed devices. Do not promise a reply service the team cannot staff.</p>
      <h2>Proposed peak broadcast and replenishment fixture</h2>
      <p>For a beauty launch, preview SMS and MMS with the actual image, link, encoding and destination mix. Confirm product variant stock, current price, code expiry and landing page before release. Exclude recent purchasers, gift recipients where appropriate, and open support cases from the proposed offer using data the account actually receives. Test those exclusions again after enrollment; a saved audience preview does not verify later dispatch state.</p><p>For a supplement store, distinguish a permitted VIP launch from replenishment based on known consumption and subscription state. Give an imported audience a permission exception cohort instead of assuming it is sendable. Record unsupported countries, incomplete identities and stale subscription events. Replenishment timing should follow the product and customer promise, not an invented industry cadence.</p>
      <h2>Cost and measurement without invented returns</h2>
      <p>Request current quotes for the same ordinary and peak-month workload: eligible profiles, email sends, SMS segments, MMS, recipient countries, carrier charges, sender costs and paid modules. Add integration maintenance and reply staffing to a split setup. Consolidation can remove a connector and shared reporting work; retaining Postscript can preserve a deliberate acquisition and reply workflow. Neither choice guarantees a conversion advantage.</p><p>Illustrative contribution math: a $100 order minus $45 product/fulfilment cost and $10 other variable cost leaves $45 before discount and messaging. A $25 discount leaves $20, so twenty genuinely incremental orders contribute $400 before $300 campaign costs, leaving $100. Thirty such orders against $450 costs leave $150. A 25% discount alone does not establish a loss. Avoid counting both discounted revenue and the discount as separate deductions.</p><p>Reconcile order IDs and attribution windows before adding dashboards together. Use an eligible randomized holdout if estimating causal lift, with uncertainty and sample limitations. Track opt-outs by flow, reply backlog, delivery failures, cost per incremental order and subsequent full-price repeat purchases; a proposed 35% attributed SMS share is not proof of lift.</p>
      <h2>Choose ownership and make cutover reversible</h2>
      <p>Choose Klaviyo when the required journeys fit its configured event/profile model and the team benefits from managing email and SMS together. Choose Postscript for an SMS program when its demonstrated capture and reply workflow meets a concrete operational need. If combining them, name the sender, queue owner, permission authority and purchase exit for every journey. Verify portability of sender, keywords and permission evidence before replacement; disable old enrollment and account for waiting messages before activating the new sender.</p><p>There is no required companion email vendor. Evaluate any alternative, including Sequenzy, against the same required events, permission records and operating workload before changing the stack. The <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-shopify-email">Shopify Email comparison</a> instead asks whether native Messaging and Flow cover the job. For further scope, see <a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-postscript">Omnisend vs Postscript</a>, <a class="link" href="/compare/postscript-vs-attentive">Postscript vs Attentive</a>, <a class="link" href="/compare/recart-vs-postscript">Recart vs Postscript</a>, <a class="link" href="/for/fashion">fashion workflows</a> and the <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">cart playbook</a>. Primary documentation checked November 25, 2026; no merchant acceptance test is claimed.</p>
    </article>
  </div></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Klaviyo vs Postscript FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
